1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,640
Imagine you're moving, and you've narrowed it down to two towns.

2
00:00:03,990 --> 00:00:05,730
Maple, and Pine Hill.

3
00:00:06,080 --> 00:00:09,287
You look up the numbers, and both towns report the same average

4
00:00:09,287 --> 00:00:10,440
household income.

5
00:00:10,790 --> 00:00:12,240
Sixty thousand dollars a year.

6
00:00:13,240 --> 00:00:16,080
Same average. So, same kind of town, right?

7
00:00:16,430 --> 00:00:18,190
Let's actually meet the families.

8
00:00:18,540 --> 00:00:19,920
Maple has nine households.

9
00:00:20,270 --> 00:00:24,622
Their incomes, in thousands of dollars, are fifty,

10
00:00:24,622 --> 00:00:28,402
fifty-two, fifty-five, fifty-eight, sixty,

11
00:00:28,402 --> 00:00:32,710
sixty-two, sixty-five, sixty-eight, and seventy.

12
00:00:33,060 --> 00:00:34,940
Everyone is fairly close to sixty.

13
00:00:35,940 --> 00:00:38,900
Now Pine Hill. Also nine households.

14
00:00:39,250 --> 00:00:42,450
Eight of them earn between thirty and forty-eight thousand.

15
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:45,720
And one family earns two hundred thirty thousand dollars a year.

16
00:00:46,720 --> 00:00:49,844
Add it all up and divide, and yes, Pine Hill's average is also

17
00:00:49,844 --> 00:00:50,380
sixty.

18
00:00:50,730 --> 00:00:52,740
But look where the families actually are.

19
00:00:53,090 --> 00:00:55,510
Eight out of nine households earn less than the average.

20
00:00:57,010 --> 00:00:59,078
The average isn't technically wrong.

21
00:00:59,078 --> 00:01:01,700
But it describes a family that doesn't live there.

22
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:06,511
What tells these towns apart is comparing two kinds of average:

23
00:01:06,511 --> 00:01:08,110
the mean, and the median.

24
00:01:09,110 --> 00:01:10,598
Welcome to Math Lab TV.

25
00:01:10,598 --> 00:01:12,720
Where math is something you can see.

26
00:01:13,720 --> 00:01:17,050
Today you will learn exactly what the mean and the median are,

27
00:01:17,050 --> 00:01:19,100
and how each one is calculated.

28
00:01:19,450 --> 00:01:23,550
You will see why one rich family can make an average lie.

29
00:01:23,900 --> 00:01:25,924
And you will learn how to pick the right one,

30
00:01:25,924 --> 00:01:26,800
every time.

31
00:01:27,800 --> 00:01:30,240
This isn't just a puzzle about two made-up towns.

32
00:01:30,590 --> 00:01:33,350
Every week, you hear averages in the news.

33
00:01:33,700 --> 00:01:36,199
Average salary. Average house price.

34
00:01:36,199 --> 00:01:38,290
Average wait time at the hospital.

35
00:01:38,640 --> 00:01:42,160
Each of those is one number standing in for thousands of people.

36
00:01:42,510 --> 00:01:45,858
If it's the wrong kind of average, it can push you into a bad

37
00:01:45,858 --> 00:01:46,630
decision.

38
00:01:46,980 --> 00:01:50,308
Like moving to Pine Hill expecting to earn what your neighbours

39
00:01:50,308 --> 00:01:50,750
earn.

40
00:01:51,100 --> 00:01:53,637
Or opening a fancy restaurant there, counting on

41
00:01:53,637 --> 00:01:56,340
sixty-thousand-dollar customers who mostly aren't there.

42
00:01:57,340 --> 00:02:00,967
The good news: telling the two averages apart takes a few

43
00:02:00,967 --> 00:02:03,010
minutes, and no fancy math.

44
00:02:03,610 --> 00:02:06,350
Let's start with the one you already know: the mean.

45
00:02:06,700 --> 00:02:09,240
The mean is what most people call 'the average'.

46
00:02:09,590 --> 00:02:13,618
To find it, add up all the values, then divide by how many there

47
00:02:13,618 --> 00:02:14,260
are.

48
00:02:14,610 --> 00:02:15,760
Let's do it for Pine Hill.

49
00:02:16,110 --> 00:02:19,257
Thirty, plus thirty-two, plus thirty-five,

50
00:02:19,257 --> 00:02:22,930
and so on, all the way up to two hundred thirty.

51
00:02:23,280 --> 00:02:24,930
The total is five hundred forty.

52
00:02:25,530 --> 00:02:27,970
There are nine households, so we divide by nine.

53
00:02:28,320 --> 00:02:30,620
Five hundred forty divided by nine is sixty.

54
00:02:31,620 --> 00:02:34,665
In math books, you'll see this written as a formula.

55
00:02:34,665 --> 00:02:37,310
Let's decode it, one symbol at a time.

56
00:02:37,660 --> 00:02:40,029
The letter x stands for one value.

57
00:02:40,029 --> 00:02:42,380
Here, one household's income.

58
00:02:42,730 --> 00:02:45,179
The small i underneath means 'which one'.

59
00:02:45,179 --> 00:02:48,961
x one is the first household, x two is the second,

60
00:02:48,961 --> 00:02:49,850
and so on.

61
00:02:50,200 --> 00:02:53,673
This big zigzag letter is the Greek capital sigma.

62
00:02:53,673 --> 00:02:55,740
It just means: add them all up.

63
00:02:56,540 --> 00:02:59,880
The letter n means how many values there are.

64
00:02:59,880 --> 00:03:01,760
For Pine Hill, n is nine.

65
00:03:02,110 --> 00:03:05,157
And the x with a bar on top, called x bar,

66
00:03:05,157 --> 00:03:06,680
is the name for the mean.

67
00:03:07,030 --> 00:03:09,782
So the whole formula says: add up every value,

68
00:03:09,782 --> 00:03:11,780
then divide by how many there are.

69
00:03:12,780 --> 00:03:14,554
But what is the mean, really?

70
00:03:14,554 --> 00:03:16,490
Here are two ways to picture it.

71
00:03:16,840 --> 00:03:19,490
Picture one: fair shares.

72
00:03:19,840 --> 00:03:22,420
Here are Pine Hill's nine incomes, as bars.

73
00:03:22,770 --> 00:03:26,710
Imagine all nine families pour their income into one shared pot.

74
00:03:27,060 --> 00:03:30,104
Then the pot is shared out equally, so every family gets the

75
00:03:30,104 --> 00:03:30,770
same amount.

76
00:03:31,120 --> 00:03:33,364
Every bar ends up at exactly sixty.

77
00:03:33,364 --> 00:03:34,090
That's the mean.

78
00:03:35,590 --> 00:03:39,043
So the mean answers one question: if everything were shared out

79
00:03:39,043 --> 00:03:40,920
evenly, how much would each get?

80
00:03:41,920 --> 00:03:44,240
Picture two: a balance beam.

81
00:03:44,590 --> 00:03:47,232
Put each household on the line as an equal weight,

82
00:03:47,232 --> 00:03:48,570
sitting at its income.

83
00:03:48,920 --> 00:03:52,120
Where should the support go, so the beam balances perfectly?

84
00:03:52,470 --> 00:03:56,666
Try forty. The beam tips to the right, because that one family

85
00:03:56,666 --> 00:03:57,770
is so far out.

86
00:03:58,120 --> 00:04:00,200
Try sixty, and it balances.

87
00:04:01,200 --> 00:04:04,670
Here's why. Measure how far each family is from sixty.

88
00:04:05,020 --> 00:04:08,654
The eight families on the left are thirty,

89
00:04:08,654 --> 00:04:12,664
twenty-eight, twenty-five, twenty-two, twenty,

90
00:04:12,664 --> 00:04:15,600
eighteen, fifteen, and twelve below.

91
00:04:15,950 --> 00:04:18,270
Together, that's one hundred seventy below.

92
00:04:18,620 --> 00:04:22,190
And the one family on the right is one hundred seventy above.

93
00:04:23,190 --> 00:04:26,362
The distances below and above cancel out exactly.

94
00:04:26,362 --> 00:04:27,960
That's what the mean always does.

95
00:04:29,460 --> 00:04:33,928
And notice: one family, far enough away, balances eight families

96
00:04:33,928 --> 00:04:34,910
all by itself.

97
00:04:36,410 --> 00:04:37,963
That's the mean's big weakness.

98
00:04:37,963 --> 00:04:41,959
Every value pulls on it, and the farther away a value is,

99
00:04:41,959 --> 00:04:43,290
the harder it pulls.

100
00:04:44,290 --> 00:04:46,880
Now, the second kind of average: the median.

101
00:04:47,230 --> 00:04:50,500
The median is the middle value, once the values are in order.

102
00:04:50,850 --> 00:04:54,900
Step one: sort. Line the incomes up from smallest to largest.

103
00:04:55,250 --> 00:04:59,320
Thirty, thirty-two, thirty-five, thirty-eight,

104
00:04:59,320 --> 00:05:03,390
forty, forty-two, forty-five, forty-eight,

105
00:05:03,390 --> 00:05:05,060
two hundred thirty.

106
00:05:05,410 --> 00:05:07,353
Step two: find the middle.

107
00:05:07,353 --> 00:05:12,520
Cross one off each end, again and again, walking inward.

108
00:05:12,870 --> 00:05:14,939
Thirty and two hundred thirty go.

109
00:05:14,939 --> 00:05:16,803
Then thirty-two and forty-eight.

110
00:05:16,803 --> 00:05:18,666
Then thirty-five and forty-five.

111
00:05:18,666 --> 00:05:20,530
Then thirty-eight and forty-two.

112
00:05:20,880 --> 00:05:23,420
One value is left standing: forty.

113
00:05:24,920 --> 00:05:28,110
The median income in Pine Hill is forty thousand dollars.

114
00:05:29,110 --> 00:05:32,050
Four families earn less, and four earn more.

115
00:05:32,050 --> 00:05:35,490
That's the median's promise: it splits the group in half.

116
00:05:36,490 --> 00:05:39,458
Compare that with the mean of sixty, where eight families were

117
00:05:39,458 --> 00:05:41,510
below and only one was above.

118
00:05:43,010 --> 00:05:47,041
In Maple, the middle value is sixty, exactly the same as its

119
00:05:47,041 --> 00:05:47,500
mean.

120
00:05:48,500 --> 00:05:51,745
You'll also hear the median called the fiftieth percentile,

121
00:05:51,745 --> 00:05:55,210
or p fifty, because it sits halfway through the sorted list.

122
00:05:55,560 --> 00:05:58,072
But what if there's an even number of values?

123
00:05:58,072 --> 00:05:59,350
Then there's no single middle.

124
00:05:59,700 --> 00:06:02,930
Say a new family moves to Pine Hill, earning fifty thousand.

125
00:06:03,280 --> 00:06:04,950
Now there are ten households.

126
00:06:05,300 --> 00:06:09,120
Cross off from both ends again, and this time two values are

127
00:06:09,120 --> 00:06:11,960
left in the middle: forty and forty-two.

128
00:06:12,310 --> 00:06:14,961
The rule: take the halfway point between them.

129
00:06:14,961 --> 00:06:17,170
Add the two, and divide by two.

130
00:06:17,520 --> 00:06:19,718
Forty plus forty-two is eighty-two.

131
00:06:19,718 --> 00:06:21,520
Half of that is forty-one.

132
00:06:22,520 --> 00:06:25,640
So with ten families, the median is forty-one thousand.

133
00:06:25,990 --> 00:06:29,185
And the mean? Five hundred ninety, divided by ten,

134
00:06:29,185 --> 00:06:30,310
which is fifty-nine.

135
00:06:30,910 --> 00:06:34,249
For the rest of this video, let's go back to our original nine

136
00:06:34,249 --> 00:06:35,020
households.

137
00:06:35,370 --> 00:06:37,611
So which average is more trustworthy?

138
00:06:37,611 --> 00:06:38,640
Let's stress test them.

139
00:06:38,990 --> 00:06:42,320
Suppose Pine Hill's richest family has an amazing year.

140
00:06:42,670 --> 00:06:46,202
Their income jumps from two hundred thirty thousand to two point

141
00:06:46,202 --> 00:06:48,290
three million. Ten times more.

142
00:06:48,640 --> 00:06:50,940
Nobody else's income changes at all.

143
00:06:51,290 --> 00:06:53,741
The new total is two thousand six hundred ten.

144
00:06:53,741 --> 00:06:57,308
Divided by nine, the mean becomes two hundred ninety thousand

145
00:06:57,308 --> 00:06:57,900
dollars.

146
00:06:58,900 --> 00:07:02,815
According to the mean, the typical family is now almost five

147
00:07:02,815 --> 00:07:06,540
times richer. Yet not one of the other eight got a raise.

148
00:07:08,040 --> 00:07:12,500
And the median? Sort, cross off, and the middle is still forty.

149
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:17,464
The median doesn't care how far away an extreme value is,

150
00:07:17,464 --> 00:07:19,770
only which side of the middle it's on.

151
00:07:20,120 --> 00:07:23,340
Values that sit far away from the rest are called outliers.

152
00:07:23,690 --> 00:07:25,827
The mean gets dragged toward outliers.

153
00:07:25,827 --> 00:07:27,963
The median hardly notices them.

154
00:07:27,963 --> 00:07:30,100
Statisticians call that being robust.

155
00:07:31,100 --> 00:07:32,669
But being robust has a cost.

156
00:07:32,669 --> 00:07:35,009
Let's go back to two hundred thirty thousand,

157
00:07:35,009 --> 00:07:38,119
and this time, the poorest family's income falls from thirty

158
00:07:38,119 --> 00:07:39,110
thousand to ten.

159
00:07:39,460 --> 00:07:41,205
Their life just got much harder.

160
00:07:41,205 --> 00:07:44,260
The mean dips, to about fifty-seven point eight.

161
00:07:44,610 --> 00:07:46,255
The median stays at forty.

162
00:07:46,255 --> 00:07:47,900
It doesn't see the change at all.

163
00:07:49,400 --> 00:07:52,810
The median ignores how far values are from the middle.

164
00:07:52,810 --> 00:07:55,157
The mean uses every bit of that.

165
00:07:55,157 --> 00:07:56,440
Each choice has a cost.

166
00:07:57,440 --> 00:08:00,770
Now let's put everything back as it was, and step back to look

167
00:08:00,770 --> 00:08:02,350
at the shape of the data.

168
00:08:02,700 --> 00:08:05,987
Maple's incomes are spread evenly around the middle.

169
00:08:05,987 --> 00:08:08,660
The left side is a mirror image of the right.

170
00:08:09,010 --> 00:08:10,365
We call that symmetric.

171
00:08:10,365 --> 00:08:13,801
And for symmetric data, the mean and the median land in the same

172
00:08:13,801 --> 00:08:14,210
spot.

173
00:08:15,210 --> 00:08:18,120
Pine Hill is lopsided: a crowd on the left,

174
00:08:18,120 --> 00:08:21,030
and a long, thin tail stretching off to the right.

175
00:08:21,380 --> 00:08:22,773
That's called right-skewed.

176
00:08:22,773 --> 00:08:26,317
And in right-skewed data, the mean usually gets pulled toward

177
00:08:26,317 --> 00:08:27,930
the tail, past the median.

178
00:08:28,930 --> 00:08:32,933
Flip it, with the tail on the left, and the mean usually lands

179
00:08:32,933 --> 00:08:34,340
below the median.

180
00:08:34,690 --> 00:08:37,740
So here's a handy warning light: if the mean is much bigger than

181
00:08:37,740 --> 00:08:41,010
the median, a few large values are probably pulling it up.

182
00:08:42,010 --> 00:08:44,787
One honest caveat: this is a rule of thumb,

183
00:08:44,787 --> 00:08:47,978
not a law. With oddly shaped data, it can fail.

184
00:08:47,978 --> 00:08:50,340
So always look at the actual values too.

185
00:08:51,340 --> 00:08:53,185
So which average should you use?

186
00:08:53,185 --> 00:08:55,210
It depends on the question you're asking.

187
00:08:55,560 --> 00:08:58,000
If your question is about totals, use the mean.

188
00:08:58,350 --> 00:09:01,405
Say Pine Hill's town council wants to plan a budget from a two

189
00:09:01,405 --> 00:09:03,050
percent local income tax.

190
00:09:03,400 --> 00:09:07,289
Total income is the mean times the number of households.

191
00:09:07,289 --> 00:09:10,490
Sixty times nine is five hundred forty thousand.

192
00:09:10,840 --> 00:09:13,484
Two percent of that is ten thousand eight hundred dollars a

193
00:09:13,484 --> 00:09:13,880
year.

194
00:09:14,230 --> 00:09:18,710
And the richest family alone pays more than forty percent of it.

195
00:09:18,710 --> 00:09:21,060
The council needs that outlier counted.

196
00:09:22,060 --> 00:09:23,516
The median can't do this.

197
00:09:23,516 --> 00:09:27,236
Forty times nine is three hundred sixty, which misses a third of

198
00:09:27,236 --> 00:09:28,280
the real total.

199
00:09:29,280 --> 00:09:32,627
But if your question is, what's life like for a typical family

200
00:09:32,627 --> 00:09:34,170
here, use the median.

201
00:09:34,520 --> 00:09:37,863
If you move to Pine Hill, forty thousand is a far better guess

202
00:09:37,863 --> 00:09:39,840
for your neighbours' income than sixty.

203
00:09:40,840 --> 00:09:43,580
And the mean and the median aren't the only options.

204
00:09:43,930 --> 00:09:46,961
There's the mode, the value that appears most often.

205
00:09:46,961 --> 00:09:50,640
But no income in Pine Hill repeats, so here there isn't one.

206
00:09:50,990 --> 00:09:54,512
There's also the trimmed mean: drop the most extreme values at

207
00:09:54,512 --> 00:09:56,810
each end, then take the mean of what's left.

208
00:09:57,160 --> 00:10:00,200
Trim one from each end of Pine Hill, and the remaining seven add

209
00:10:00,200 --> 00:10:01,581
up to two hundred eighty.

210
00:10:01,581 --> 00:10:03,680
Divide by seven, and you get forty.

211
00:10:04,680 --> 00:10:07,349
Here it happens to match the median exactly.

212
00:10:07,349 --> 00:10:11,462
In general, a trimmed mean sits somewhere between the two ideas:

213
00:10:11,462 --> 00:10:15,780
it uses most of the values, but one extreme one can't hijack it.

214
00:10:16,130 --> 00:10:19,332
And when in doubt, report both the mean and the median.

215
00:10:19,332 --> 00:10:21,890
The gap between them tells you something too.

216
00:10:22,890 --> 00:10:25,423
This choice shows up everywhere data is used.

217
00:10:25,423 --> 00:10:26,710
Let's visit four places.

218
00:10:27,060 --> 00:10:28,320
First, money.

219
00:10:28,670 --> 00:10:32,149
Incomes and house prices usually have a long right tail,

220
00:10:32,149 --> 00:10:35,196
just like Pine Hill: lots of ordinary values,

221
00:10:35,196 --> 00:10:36,720
and a few huge ones.

222
00:10:37,070 --> 00:10:40,513
That's why the U.S. Census Bureau headlines median household

223
00:10:40,513 --> 00:10:41,960
income, not the mean.

224
00:10:42,310 --> 00:10:46,130
And why housing reports usually quote the median sale price.

225
00:10:46,480 --> 00:10:49,678
If a company advertises its average salary,

226
00:10:49,678 --> 00:10:51,573
ask whether the boss's pay is in there.

227
00:10:51,573 --> 00:10:54,772
One huge salary can lift the mean, just like Pine Hill's richest

228
00:10:54,772 --> 00:10:55,550
family.

229
00:10:56,550 --> 00:10:58,284
Next, computer science.

230
00:10:58,284 --> 00:11:01,870
Think about how long a website takes to respond.

231
00:11:02,220 --> 00:11:05,827
Most requests are quick, but now and then one gets stuck and

232
00:11:05,827 --> 00:11:06,950
takes ages.

233
00:11:07,300 --> 00:11:09,157
It's the Pine Hill pattern again.

234
00:11:09,157 --> 00:11:12,857
A few slow requests drag the mean up, so it no longer describes

235
00:11:12,857 --> 00:11:14,100
a normal visit.

236
00:11:14,450 --> 00:11:18,230
So engineers track the median, which they call p fifty,

237
00:11:18,230 --> 00:11:22,487
alongside the slow tail, such as p ninety-nine: the time that

238
00:11:22,487 --> 00:11:25,570
ninety-nine percent of requests beat or match.

239
00:11:26,570 --> 00:11:29,642
In data science, one of the first jobs with new data is cleaning

240
00:11:29,642 --> 00:11:30,030
it.

241
00:11:30,380 --> 00:11:33,970
Suppose the richest family's income was typed with one extra

242
00:11:33,970 --> 00:11:37,181
zero: two point three million instead of two hundred thirty

243
00:11:37,181 --> 00:11:37,800
thousand.

244
00:11:38,150 --> 00:11:41,977
As we saw, that one typo drags the mean to two hundred ninety

245
00:11:41,977 --> 00:11:43,820
thousand. The median doesn't flinch.

246
00:11:44,820 --> 00:11:47,962
So data scientists compare the mean and median as a quick check

247
00:11:47,962 --> 00:11:49,360
for typos and outliers.

248
00:11:49,360 --> 00:11:52,329
And when filling in missing values in skewed data,

249
00:11:52,329 --> 00:11:54,120
they often use the median.

250
00:11:54,470 --> 00:11:56,292
Finally, machine learning.

251
00:11:56,292 --> 00:11:59,890
Here, the choice hides inside something called a loss function.

252
00:12:00,240 --> 00:12:03,641
Imagine a model that must make one single guess for every Pine

253
00:12:03,641 --> 00:12:05,970
Hill family's income. What's the best guess?

254
00:12:06,320 --> 00:12:08,450
It depends on how you score the mistakes.

255
00:12:08,800 --> 00:12:12,640
Score one: add up how far off the guess is for each family.

256
00:12:12,640 --> 00:12:14,560
That's called absolute error.

257
00:12:14,910 --> 00:12:18,407
Slide the guess along, and the total distance is smallest at

258
00:12:18,407 --> 00:12:19,940
forty. The median.

259
00:12:20,940 --> 00:12:23,659
Score two: square each distance before adding,

260
00:12:23,659 --> 00:12:25,475
so big misses count extra.

261
00:12:25,475 --> 00:12:27,290
That's called squared error.

262
00:12:27,640 --> 00:12:29,968
Now the best guess is sixty.

263
00:12:29,968 --> 00:12:30,790
The mean.

264
00:12:31,790 --> 00:12:34,569
This works for any data, not just Pine Hill.

265
00:12:34,569 --> 00:12:37,341
Minimising squared error lands on the mean.

266
00:12:37,341 --> 00:12:40,538
Minimising absolute error lands on the median,

267
00:12:40,538 --> 00:12:44,380
or with an even count, anywhere between the two middle values.

268
00:12:45,880 --> 00:12:49,158
So when someone trains a model with mean squared error or mean

269
00:12:49,158 --> 00:12:52,860
absolute error, they're choosing whether it aims for a mean or a

270
00:12:52,860 --> 00:12:53,490
median.

271
00:12:53,840 --> 00:12:56,165
Squared error chases outliers.

272
00:12:56,165 --> 00:12:58,491
Absolute error shrugs them off.

273
00:12:58,491 --> 00:13:00,510
Same trade-off, new setting.

274
00:13:01,510 --> 00:13:04,130
Before we wrap up, here are the three most common mistakes

275
00:13:04,130 --> 00:13:05,400
people make with averages.

276
00:13:05,750 --> 00:13:08,600
Mistake one: taking the middle without sorting first.

277
00:13:08,950 --> 00:13:13,641
Here are Pine Hill's incomes in the order the houses sit on the

278
00:13:13,641 --> 00:13:17,649
street: forty-two, two hundred thirty, thirty-five,

279
00:13:17,649 --> 00:13:21,396
forty-eight, thirty, forty-five, thirty-eight,

280
00:13:21,396 --> 00:13:23,400
thirty-two, and forty.

281
00:13:23,750 --> 00:13:25,647
The middle of that list is thirty.

282
00:13:25,647 --> 00:13:28,800
That's not the median. It's just whoever lives in the middle

283
00:13:28,800 --> 00:13:29,230
house.

284
00:13:30,230 --> 00:13:33,940
Always sort first. Sorted, the middle is forty.

285
00:13:34,290 --> 00:13:37,450
Mistake two: assuming half the people are above the average.

286
00:13:37,800 --> 00:13:40,166
That's true for the median, by design.

287
00:13:40,166 --> 00:13:41,670
It's not true for the mean.

288
00:13:42,020 --> 00:13:45,757
In Pine Hill, only one family out of nine is above the mean.

289
00:13:45,757 --> 00:13:49,310
So if you're below average there, you're in very good company.

290
00:13:50,310 --> 00:13:53,258
Mistake three: deciding the median is always better,

291
00:13:53,258 --> 00:13:55,270
and that outliers should just be deleted.

292
00:13:55,620 --> 00:13:57,418
That rich family is real.

293
00:13:57,418 --> 00:14:01,245
Their income is real money, and the town's tax budget depends on

294
00:14:01,245 --> 00:14:01,690
it.

295
00:14:02,040 --> 00:14:05,708
Remove a value only when you have evidence it's an error,

296
00:14:05,708 --> 00:14:09,812
like that extra zero. Otherwise keep it, and choose the average

297
00:14:09,812 --> 00:14:11,110
that fits your question.

298
00:14:12,110 --> 00:14:15,691
And a bonus mistake: with an even count, picking one of the two

299
00:14:15,691 --> 00:14:18,180
middle numbers instead of the halfway point between them.

300
00:14:18,530 --> 00:14:22,185
Let's turn all of that into a recipe you can use on any set of

301
00:14:22,185 --> 00:14:22,790
numbers.

302
00:14:23,140 --> 00:14:26,930
Step one: sort the values, from smallest to largest.

303
00:14:27,280 --> 00:14:28,849
Step two: find the mean.

304
00:14:28,849 --> 00:14:32,450
Add them all up, and divide by how many there are.

305
00:14:32,800 --> 00:14:35,534
Step three: find the median, the middle value.

306
00:14:35,534 --> 00:14:39,200
With an even count, take the halfway point of the middle two.

307
00:14:39,550 --> 00:14:41,158
Step four: compare them.

308
00:14:41,158 --> 00:14:45,102
If they're close, the data is probably fairly balanced.

309
00:14:45,102 --> 00:14:49,280
If the mean is much bigger, look for large values pulling it up.

310
00:14:49,630 --> 00:14:52,943
Step five: match the average to your question.

311
00:14:52,943 --> 00:14:55,472
Totals and fair shares: the mean.

312
00:14:55,472 --> 00:14:58,784
A typical person or thing: the median.

313
00:14:58,784 --> 00:15:00,530
Not sure? Report both.

314
00:15:02,030 --> 00:15:05,760
For Pine Hill, the mean is sixty, and the median is forty.

315
00:15:05,760 --> 00:15:08,860
Both are correct. They answer different questions.

316
00:15:09,860 --> 00:15:13,110
An average only lies when you ask it the wrong question.

317
00:15:14,610 --> 00:15:17,897
Look around your world, and notice which kind of average people

318
00:15:17,897 --> 00:15:18,730
quote to you.

319
00:15:19,330 --> 00:15:21,811
If this lesson made things clear for you,

320
00:15:21,811 --> 00:15:25,296
please subscribe to Math Lab TV, so we can keep building

321
00:15:25,296 --> 00:15:26,270
together.

322
00:15:27,070 --> 00:15:28,550
But here's a puzzle for next time.

323
00:15:28,900 --> 00:15:31,405
Two towns can have the same mean and the same median,

324
00:15:31,405 --> 00:15:34,290
and still be completely different places to live.

325
00:15:34,640 --> 00:15:38,318
The missing piece is spread: how far values typically sit from

326
00:15:38,318 --> 00:15:38,970
the middle.

327
00:15:39,320 --> 00:15:42,365
Next time, we'll measure it with the standard deviation,

328
00:15:42,365 --> 00:15:44,790
and see why it squares the distances first.

329
00:15:45,140 --> 00:15:46,640
I will see you in the next video.
